<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/associate-professor-damien-holloway-0796109cc159> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Associate Professor Damien Holloway"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-engineering-757090ead0c4> ;
    utas:bio "Damien is an Associate Professor and Associate Head Learning and Teaching in Engineering. He is an alumnus of the University of Tasmania with a University Medal, and is interested in architectural acoustics, structural dynamics, vibration, buckling and ship hydrodynamics.BiographyDamien has undergraduate degrees in both Civil Engineering (for which he was awarded a University Medal) and Music, both from the University of Tasmania, and has completed a Master of Music Qualifying at the University of Queensland. His PhD (University of Tasmania) applied the boundary element method to the time domain study of free surface waves and ship dynamics. He has held postdoctoral positions in the School of Mathematics and Physics and the School of Engineering at the University of Tasmania, studying groundwater flows and ship structural dynamics respectively. Prior to his engineering career he worked as a violist in several of Australia's leading professional orchestras.Damien was appointed lecturer in Engineering at the University of Tasmania in 2005 where he has over time taught the majority of subjects within the Civil and Mechanical engineering degrees, and has received two teaching merit certificates. In recent years his teaching has been predominantly in the areas of structural mechanics, stress analysis and vibrations.Damien has authored or co-authored over 130 refereed journal and conference papers relating to buckling, ship dynamics and architectural acoustics. The latter exploits his unique combination of qualifications and experience in engineering, music and mathematics.He has been the Associate Head Learning and Teaching for the School of Engineering since 2019. He has also been Engineering Honours coordinator since 2007, undergraduate degree coordinator since 2011 and was Deputy Head of School 2012-13.Career summaryQualificationsPh.D. A high Froude number time domain strip theory applied to the seakeeping of semi-SWATHs, University of Tasmania, Australia 1999BE (Hons) with University Medal: A potential flow solution of the weir overfall problem by inverse mapping, University of Tasmania, Australia 1993BMus Aspects of intonation control in viola playing, University of Tasmania, Australia 1986Master of Music Qualifying (MMusQual), University of Queensland, Australia 1986Graduate Diploma of Professional Music Practice, University of Tasmania, Australia, 2014MembershipsProfessional practiceMember Australian Mathematical SocietyMember, Engineers AustraliaHonorary Fellow, Jane Franklin Hall (a residential college of the University of Tasmania)Administrative expertiseAwarded Commendation for Service Excellence to College of Sciences and Engineering, 20202019-2024: Associate Head Learning and Teaching, School of Engineering2015-2024: Board president/vice president, Musica Viva Tasmania2012-2013: Deputy Head, School of Engineering2011-2022 & 2026-present: Course Coordinator, Engineering2008-present: Honours Coordinator, Engineering"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Associate Professor Damien Holloway"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Damien.Holloway"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Damien.Holloway"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Damien.Holloway@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/acoustics-and-acoustical-devices-waves-23dd668f84dc>,
