<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/associate-professor-kate-booth-5ed6d22b93ef> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Associate Professor Kate Booth"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-geography-planning-and-spatial-sciences-de1e76643cc9> ;
    utas:bio "Kate Booth is a human geographer and activist academic. Her work addresses socio-ecological inequity in the context of rapid and unprecedented global change.She leads the Just Collapse platform - addressing global collapse with a commitment to socio-ecological theory, equitable change, and emancipatory politics. This platform mixes academic research, education, and activism, and leverages Kate's research investigating geographies of insurance and society in a changing climate. About her new book, In Collapse: When Insurance and Climate Collide - \"In Collapse unsettles. Booth forces us to turn and face, head-on, the many discomfiting truths about living in and with climate breakdown. But it also clarifies and empowers. With their critical interrogation of collapse through the prism of insurance, we better know what worlds may be ending, which already have, and how to look for solidarity amidst the wreckage. The analysis here provides a powerful rethinking of what collective risk sharing and survival might mean and require.\" Associate Professor Rebecca Elliott, Department of Sociology, The London School of Economics and Political ScienceHer teaching also addresses rising inequity and the escalating economic and social impacts and risks of unmitigated climate change. In 2021, Kate led the successful reaccreditation of the University’s Master of Planning by the Planning Institute of Australia. As part of the Masters, she co-ordinates KGA742/3 Research Thesis A/B, and co-ordinates and teaches KGA521 Urban Planning and Design and KGA705 Climate Change: Economics, Politics and Planning. Kate is Lead Graduate Research Coordinator in the School of Geography, Planning, and Spatial Sciences - supporting the School, supervisors, and PhD students in achieving research priorities, quality researcher training, and best practice outcomes. Awards Regional Studies Association Best Paper Award - Finance and Space (2025) Commendation for Outstanding Teaching - KGA521 (2025, 2024, 2022, 2020); KGA705 (2024, 2022) College Sustainability Award (2021) Dean’s Award for Outstanding Research Performance by a Level A/B Researcher (2017) Deans Citation for outstanding academic performance in undergraduate and Honours courses (2006) W.D. Jackson prize for greatest proficiency 3rd-year Plant Science (1993) Dean's Roll of Excellence (1991, 1992, 1993)"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Associate Professor Kate Booth"^^xsd:string ;
