<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-andreas-seger-89fd9b4aab81> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Andreas Seger"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/institute-for-marine-and-antarctic-studies-bd99c2807811> ;
    utas:bio "Biotoxin management and harmful algal bloom expertise Andreas is an internationally recognized expert in marine biotoxins, harmful algal bloom management and seafood safety. He is a Senior Research Fellow in the Health Seafood research group at the Institute for Marine and Antarctic Studies. His research focuses on understanding key physiological and cellular processes in harmful microalgae and applying this knowledge to actively improve marine biotoxin risk management and biosecurity. He provides scientific advice to SafeFish, the National Seafood Safety and Market Access program and coordinates the Australian ciguatera poisoning working group. His research directly informs risk management practices to safeguard commercially important finfish, lobster and abalone fisheries.BiographyAndreas completed his PhD at the University of Tasmania in 2017, where he explored the use of clay minerals to successfully mitigate the impact of fish-killing algal blooms. Building upon his microalgal expertise, Andreas moved to the South Australian Research and Development Institute in Adelaide, where he investigated marine biotoxins of human health significance. During his appointment as Senior Research Officer in seafood safety, Andreas conducted fundamental research into the uptake of paralytic shellfish toxins by oysters, rock lobsters and abalone to inform biotoxin risk management and validate rapid toxin test kits. He has developed a unique, high volume toxic microalgal culturing system that has been the cornerstone of applied biotoxin research in Australia, directly informing revisions of biotoxin risk management plans through improved knowledge of biotoxin uptake pathways and mechanisms. At the same time, Andreas provides scientific advice to the SafeFish seafood safety program to prioritise future research directions and coordinated the development of the Australian research strategy for ciguatera poisoning.In 2020, Andreas joined the University of Tasmania as Research Fellow in Fish Health, Biosecurity and Seafood Safety. His current work predominantly focuses on improved detection and management of paralytic shellfish toxins in Tasmanian waters. At the same time, he is building capability to quantify fish-killing algal toxins and other unknown aquatic toxicants with the use of novel cell line techniques. He continues to provide scientific advice to the SafeFish seafood safety partnership and has launched a national ciguatera awareness campaign.Memberships/Professional practiceInternational Society for the Study of Harmful Algae (ISSHA)Tasmanian Shellfish Aquaculture Research Advisory Group Expert Roster of the Joint (FAO/WHO) International consultant on Harmful Algal Blooms and Marine BiotoxinsEditorial board of the Journal of Harmful Algae Academic qualificationsPhD \"Exploration of clay minerals to mitigate fish-killing algal blooms.\" University of Tasmania, Australia, 19/08/2017.BSc (1st Class Hons) \"Ichthyotoxicity by the tropical microalga Prymnesium parvum: Implications for aquaculture fish pond management.\" University of Tasmania, Australia, 12/12/2011.BSc University of Tasmania Australia 13/12/2010Languages (other than English)German (fluent)"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Andreas Seger"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Andreas.Seger"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Andreas.Seger"^^xsd:string ;
