<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-brian-salmon-981747e44480> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Brian Salmon"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-engineering-757090ead0c4> ;
    utas:bio "Biography:Dr Brian Salmon received his PhD degree in electronic engineering from the University of Pretoria, South Africa in 2012. He is an electronic engineer and lecturer at the University of Tasmania. Before joining the University of Tasmania, Brian was a senior researcher at the Council for Scientific and Industrial Research where he initiated and led new projects in maritime domain awareness. These projects included collaboration from institutions such as the Office of Naval Research Global (ONRG - USA), Space and Naval Warfare Systems Command (SPAWAR - USA), Defence Peace Safety and Security (DPSS - South Africa). These projects were part of an International Collaborative Development for Enhanced Maritime Domain Awareness program that was funded by the Office of the Under Secretary of Defence for Acquisitions, Technology and Logistics for a coalition warfare program.In addition, he has been part of the Remote Sensing Research Unit for the past 7 years and researched and developed numerous remote sensing prototypes that were later delivered as products to various industries, government departments, banking sectors and fire protection.He has years of experience in interdisciplinary projects working in large and small teams. Industry projects include medical (Menzies Institute (Hobart), Delft University and Leiden University Medical Centre (Netherlands), and University of Tuebingen (Germany)), telecommunication (BWMC (RSA), Sentech Labs (RSA)), remote sensing (Remote Sensing Centre (QLD), CSIR (RSA), Department of Science and Technology (RSA government), Munus International (RSA)), maritime domain awareness (ICODE-MDA, ONRG USA) and seismology (Institute of Mine Seismology (Hobart)) to mention only a few.He has formed numerous Tasmanian industry connections (INCAT, IMS, CSIRO Hobart, Jayben,etc.), national connections (Remote Sensing Centre (QLD), OpenGear (NSW), DSTG (NSW)) and international connections (ONRG (USA), SPAWAR (USA), CSIR (RSA), South African Mining Council (RSA), Munus International (RSA), Leiden University Medical Centre (Netherlands), and University of Tuebingen (Germany)).He continually grows his many Tasmanian connections as work placement coordinator for the School of Engineering. His research interest and expertise are in control theory, signal processing, information theory, artificial intelligence (deep learning), communication systems, and embedded system design. He have been awarded best paper award at the top conference in my field (IGARSS), with participation of NASA, ESA and JAXA. He has been invited to present his work on several occasions and to host panel sessions. His research interfaces with other engineering disciplines including mining, structural, biomedical, mechanical, as well as geography computing. He has several patents pending, commercialisation/grants and numerous honours and PhD students. He is an IEEE member and was a guest Editor for the IEEE Journal of Selected Topics of Remote Sensing.Career summary:PhD - Improved hyper-temporal feature extraction methods for land cover change detection in satellite time seriesM.Eng - Optimizing LDPC codes for mobile WiMAX system with a saturated transmission amplifierB.Eng (1st class Hons) - Electronic EngineeringMembershipsProfessional practice 2008 - : Golden Key International Honours Society 2006 - : IEEE memberAdministrative expertiseEditorial Guest editor for IEEE Journal of Selected Topics in Applied Earth Observations and Remote Sensing (JSTARS) 2013-2015Organising committees Session organiser – Hyper-temporal, image analysis and change detection session. IEEE International Geoscience and Remote Sensing Symposium 2014 Symposium committee – IEEE Information Theory Workshop 2014"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Brian Salmon"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Brian.Salmon"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Brian.Salmon"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Brian.Salmon@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/artificial-intelligence-803b3eb2b6af>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/biomedical-engineering-5612bbff6336>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/communications-engineering-0cc8766c323a>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/electronics-sensors-and-digital-hardware-64539b4bb20f>,
