<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-brooklyn-fraser-3a4e6da6d280> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Brooklyn Fraser"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/university-of-tasmania-aef6a7aa1611> ;
    utas:bio "Dr Brooklyn Fraser is a Heart Foundation Postdoctoral Research Fellow. Her research is focused on the health and fitness of Australian children, with a strong interest in the long-term cardiovascular health benefits of greater fitness. Brooklyn completed a Bachelor of Biotechnology and Medical Research with First Class Honours in 2015. Brooklyn received her PhD from the University of Tasmania in 2019. Brooklyn’s PhD explored the association between childhood muscular fitness and type 2 diabetes related outcomes in adulthood. Her research helped identify muscular fitness, in childhood and across the life course, as a target to help prevent type 2 diabetes. Brooklyn was awarded the Menzies Institute for Medical Research Postdoctoral Fellowship in 2020 and a nationally competitive Heart Foundation Postdoctoral Fellowship in 2022. Brooklyn’s postdoctoral research examines the health and fitness of Australian children, with a strong focus on the long-term cardiovascular health benefits of greater fitness. Brooklyn works with large observational studies with longitudinal data and is experienced with analytic techniques relevant to longitudinal data including life-course modelling. Brooklyn has independently developed highly engaged, impactful and ongoing international collaborations with leading paediatric exercise science experts, formalised within the Global Youth Fitness Consortium (>100 international collaborators). Brooklyn's research vision is to establish and champion the protective power of childhood fitness to enable children to lead fit and heart-healthy lives into adulthood. She aims for her research to inform public health and clinical guidelines, early prevention strategies, and school‑based programs that strengthen child fitness."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Brooklyn Fraser"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Brooklyn.Fraser"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Brooklyn.Fraser"^^xsd:string ;
