<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-damien-kingston-85fb51bfdf60> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Damien Kingston"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/university-of-tasmania-aef6a7aa1611> ;
    utas:bio "Damien Kingston is a Hobart-born guitarist, composer, researcher, and educator whose practice spans performance, recording, curriculum leadership, and community engagement across jazz, improvised music, and contemporary creative practice. After completing a Bachelor of Music at the University of Tasmania, Damien relocated to Europe, working as a freelance musician and completing a Masters of Music at the Conservatorium van Amsterdam (2013) under Dutch guitar luminaries Martijn van Iterson and Jesse van Ruller, following an active period in Berlin's jazz and improvised music community. Returning to Tasmania, Damien completed a PhD at the University of Tasmania (2018) and joined the Conservatorium as a casual lecturer in 2014 before moving to ongoing employment in 2019. He currently holds three formal leadership positions - Associate Head Learning and Teaching, Course Coordinator, and Honours Coordinator - with accountability for the Bachelor of Music, Diploma of Music, Associate Degree, and Honours award courses. His teaching philosophy integrates academic rigour, industry authenticity, and inclusive practice, supporting students from diverse backgrounds to develop professional skills and discover their own artistic voice. As a performer and recording artist, Damien has released seven albums of original compositions and contributed to more than thirty commercial releases across jazz, experimental, and contemporary popular music. His 2021 ABC-commissioned album Wells reached audiences across more than one hundred countries and was selected as Album of the Week on ABC Jazz Radio. His most recent release, Let the Light In - Damien Kingston Quartet (2023), was released by Made Now Music. Damien has performed internationally both as a bandleader and sideman, and received awards including first place in the 2016 Berlin Jazz & Blues Awards and a finalist placement in the 2013 Keep an Eye European Jazz Awards. Beyond the institution, Damien is a committed builder of Tasmania's creative ecosystem - founding the Pablo's Jazz Jam Session (now in its seventh year), co-founding Scratch Match Records, leading the Conservatorium's partnership with the Clarence Jazz Festival. QualificationsPhD, Conservatorium of Music, University of Tasmania, Australia, 2018. Exegesis: Free Improvisation in the Context of Repertoire Interpretation - An Applied Investigation of Derek Bailey’s Ballads.M.Mus, Conservatorium van Amsterdam, Amsterdamse Hogeschool voor de Kunsten, Netherlands, 2013. Thesis: The Keith Jarrett American Quartet - Harmonic Improvisation and Collective Rubato.B.Mus, Conservatorium van Amsterdam, Amsterdamse Hogeschool voor de Kunsten, Netherlands, 2011. B.Mus, Conservatorium of Music, University of Tasmania, Australia, 2005."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Damien Kingston"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Damien.Kingston"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Damien.Kingston"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Damien.Kingston@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/creative-arts-and-writing-62daf37a74cd>,
