    utas:updatedWhen "2026-09-12T03:35:36.0805405+10:00"^^xsd:string .

<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-gabriela-bodea-2d7cc627e157> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Gabriela Bodea"@en ;
    utas:bio "Dr Gabriela Bodea is a Senior Lecturer in Biomedicine and Head of the Molecular Neurogenetics Lab at the Tasmanian School of Medicine, University of Tasmania. Dr Bodea completed her PhD with the highest distinction, summa cum laude, at the University of Bonn (2014, Germany). Dr Bodea subsequently pursued postdoctoral training at the Mater Research Institute and then at the Queensland Brain Institute, University of Queensland, Australia. During this time, Dr Bodea's work significantly contributed to understanding the role of LINE-1 retrotransposons, a class of mobile DNA elements colloquially known as “jumping genes” in generating genetic diversity within neurons during mammalian neurodevelopment (Cell, 2015; Genome Research 2017, 2022, 2023; Nature Neuroscience, 2024). In mid-2025, Dr Bodea relocated to the University of Tasmania, assuming a teaching and research role and establishing the Molecular Neurogenetics Research Lab. Dr Bodea's Molecular Neurogenetics (MNG) Research Lab investigates the mechanisms of neural plasticity, specifically how environmental exposures shape the genetic identity of neurons and direct the formation of mammalian brain circuitry, enabling adaptive processes. The MNG team also aims to understand how environmental factors, immune signalling, and genetic predisposition contribute to complex neurodevelopmental and neurodegenerative conditions, such as schizophrenia and Parkinson’s disease. To address these questions, the MNG team applies a powerful, integrated toolkit spanning cellular and molecular biology, neuroscience, and genomics. This multidisciplinary approach enables the MNG team to precisely dissect gene-regulatory mechanisms and delineate the cellular and molecular pathways that drive vulnerability and resilience in the central nervous system. Our research has secured prestigious and highly competitive funding, including fellowships from the German Research Foundation (DFG, 2014-2016), the NHMRC-ARC Dementia Research Development (2016-2020), and the Brain & Behavior Research Foundation (BBRF) Young Investigator Award (2025-2027). Reflecting the impact of our work, our findings have been published in top-tier journals, including Nature Neuroscience, Cell Reports, Genome Research, and Development. Methods and Techniques of the Molecular Neurogenetics Lab Mouse models (transgene, conditional gene inactivation and overexpression via CRISPRi/a, genetic inducible fate mapping) Histology (paraffin- and cryosectioning, RNA in situ hybridisation, immunohistochemistry) Cell culture (induced pluripotent stem cells, primary neuronal cultures, organotypic slice culture, organoid and assembloid 3D cultures) Genomics and transcriptomics (RNAseq, DNA and RNA long-read sequencing) Epigenetic tools (CUT&RUN, CUT&Tag) Molecular biology (PCR, RNA and DNA analysis, gene cloning, qPCR) Microscopy (confocal microscopy, fluorescence live imaging)"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Gabriela Bodea"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Gabriela.Bodea"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Gabriela.Bodea"^^xsd:string ;
