<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-gary-morris-a40da5f760e3> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Gary Morris"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/university-of-tasmania-aef6a7aa1611> ;
    utas:bio "Dr. Gary Morris is a lecturer within the Wicking Dementia Research and Education Centre. He completed his PhD in 2017 at the University of New South Wales, before undertaking postdoctoral research at University of Technology, Sydney, from 2017-2019. In 2019, Dr. Morris joined the Perivascular Research Group at the University of Tasmania, led by Professor Brad Sutherland, as a Research Fellow. During this time he worked on projects related to comparative neuroanatomy, physiological neurovascular function, stroke, Alzheimer's disease, vascular dementia and multiple sclerosis. In 2025, Dr. Morris began his research group, the Gliovascular Research Group, within the Wicking Dementia Research and Education Centre at UTAS. His research investigates how non-neuronal cells residing within the brain, and around the vasculature of the brain, contribute to vascular functions including the control of brain blood flow, blood-brain-barrier protection and brain waste removal. His research aims to understand how the gliovascular unit functions in the healthy brain, but also how it breaks down and thereby contributes to microvascular dysfunction in multiple neurological conditions, with a focus on dementia and stroke. Dr. Morris teaches into several undergraduate neuroscience and biochemistry units within the Wicking Dementia Research and Education Centre and the Tasmanian School of Medicine. He also supervises several postgraduate research students."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Gary Morris"^^xsd:string ;
