<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-hong-liu-4f7dda3889a2> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Hong Liu"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-engineering-757090ead0c4> ;
    utas:bio "Dr Hongyuan Liu is currently a senior lecturer in geomechanics at the School of Engineering in the College of Sciences and Engineering, University of Tasmania and an affiliated research scientist at CSIRO Mineral Resources. Hong's research interest is in the field of geohazard mitigation and prevention, computational geomechanics, tunnelling, and rock mechanics. In particular, Hong's research focuses on the development and application of advanced computational methods such as hybrid finite-discrete element method (HFDEM) in studying the fracture and fragmentation of rocks under various static and dynamic loads during tunnelling and mining, associated microseismicities, and their interactions with underground fluids such as groundwater, oil, gas and hazardous waste as well as mitigating and even preventing geohazards such as rockbursts, slope instabilities and iceberg calving. For example, Hong recently developed HFDEM and founded its website: https://sites.google.com/view/hfdem/home. Together with his team, Hong parallelised HFDEM on the basis of general-purpose graphic-processing-units (GPGPU) using compute unified device architecture (CUDA) C/C++ and developed a number of novel and efficient features, which have made HFDEM become a powerful numerical tool for investigating the fracture and fragmentation of rocks under static and dynamic loading conditions as well as mitigating and preventing the instability and collapse of rockmass structures and resultant debris fragmentation and flow process. Hong's and his team's series of research on the development and application of HFDEM has claimed a series of awards including the Top 15 Highest Ranked Paper in the 12th Australia - New Zealand Conference on Geomechanics in 2012, the Best PhD Student Paper Awards in the 3rd and 4th Australasian Conference on Computational Mechanics in 2018 and 2019, respectively, the only Excellent Paper Award in the 5th ISRM Young Scholar's Symposium on Rock Mechanics and International Symposium on Rock Engineering for Innovative Future in 2019 and the Excellent Paper Award in the 11th Asian Rock Mechanics Symposium in 2020. HFDEM is free to use for the purpose of research and/or verification although the use of the code for military and commercial purpose is prohibited. If interested, please download HFDEM and know more information from our website: https://sites.google.com/view/hfdem/home. Hong's research has resulted in more than 100 peer-reviewed journal papers including many in the leading geomechanics journals such as International Journal of Rock Mechanics and Mining Sciences, Rock Mechanics and Rock Engineering, and Computers and Geotechnics, which have attracted a citation number of 3088 and 4111 citations and an h-index of 32 and 36 according to Scopus and Google Scholar, respectively, till June 2023.BiographyDr Hongyuan Liu's current research at UTAS encompasses the broad areas of geohazard mitigation and prevention, computational geomechanics, tunnelling and rock mechanics. He is the academic manager of Geomechanics Lab at UTAS and teaches ENG221 Engineering Geology and Soil Mechanics, ENG315/706 Foundation Engineering and Design and ENG717 Rock Mechanics and Rock Engineering.Hong was a research fellow at the Golder Geomechanics Centre led by Prof David Williams within the University of Queensland before being appointed in UTAS in 2010. Prior to joining in UQ in 2008, Hong was a postdoctoral research fellow at the University of Sydney funded by an ARC Discovery Project 'Effects of Tunnelling on Existing Support System' held by Profs John Small and John Carter. Hong completed his PhD at Lulea University of Technology, Sweden in 2004 and later worked there as a research scientist till moving to Australia in 2006. During the period as a research scientist, Hong, together with Profs Per-Arne Lindqvist and SQ Kou, attracted research grants of over $1M SEK from LKAB, Atlas Copco, SKB's Aspo Hard Rock Laboratory and SGU. Prior to being awarded PhD scholarships by LTU Sweden in 2001, Hong completed his Masters and Bachelor degrees at Northeastern University China in 2000 and 1997, respectively, during which, he was the early developer of the RFPA code led by Cheung Kong Professor Tang.Career summaryQualifications2004: PhD, Lulea University of Technology, Sweden 20042000: ME, Northeastern University, China1997: BE, Northeastern University, China 1997Languages (other than English)Chinese, SwedishMemberships:Committee associationsFellow of Institute of Engineers AustraliaMember of International Society of Rock MechanicsMember of Australian Geomechanics SocietyMember of Australian Institute of Mining and MetallurgyEditorial Board Members of International Journal of Rock Mechanics and Mining Science, Underground Space and other 3 SCI-indexed journalsRegular peer reviewer for more than 10 international journals including International Journal of Rock Mechanics and Mining Sciences, Rock Mechanics and Rock Engineering, Computers and Geotechnics, and Tunnelling and Underground Space Technology"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Hong Liu"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Hong.Liu"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Hong.Liu"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Hong.Liu@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/civil-engineering-418be4b707cd>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/civil-geotechnical-engineering-335161e3e154>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/engineering-8c85b6639e62> ;
    utas:orcid "0000-0002-5437-4695"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:researchFieldsText "Civil geotechnical engineering; Civil engineering; Engineering"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:school "School of Engineering"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:supervises <https://example.org/utas-research/project/12307> ;
    utas:title "Doctor"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:updatedWhen "2026-09-16T16:38:24.4062084+10:00"^^xsd:string .

