<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-jane-younger-63f148f4453b> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Jane Younger"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/institute-for-marine-and-antarctic-studies-bd99c2807811> ;
    utas:bio "Dr Jane Younger is a Senior Lecturer in Southern Ocean Vertebrate Ecology at the Institute for Marine and Antarctic Studies (IMAS). Her research integrates an Antarctic field program with cutting edge multi-omics approaches to monitor the complex impacts of climate change on Antarctic biodiversity, with a particular focus on understanding the threat of disease to wildlife in a changing environment. She is a 2025-26 Science and Technology Australia Superstar of STEM, a Senior Editor of Ecology and Evolution, a National Geographic Explorer, and a 2025 Young Tall Poppy Award winner.Prior to joining IMAS in 2022, Jane was a research fellow in the Milner Centre for Evolution at the University of Bath (UK). Her research focused on understanding the impacts of climate change on seabirds, through an evolutionary lens. She has also held an Endeavour Fellowship at the University of Oxford (UK) where she used genomics to examine the population structure of Southern Ocean penguin species. Prior to that she held a postdoctoral position at Loyola University Chicago (USA) and in affiliation with the Field Museum of Natural History, studying the evolution of Madagascar’s endemic avifauna. Jane completed her PhD in 2016 at IMAS, where she focused on the past impacts of climate change on Southern Ocean marine predators. Jane’s published work spans the fields of ecology, conservation, evolution, genomics, and wildlife health. The common theme throughout her career has been understanding how environmental change effects biodiversity. Expertise Wildlife health of seabirds and marine mammalsApplying genomic tools to understand the impacts of climate change on marine predatorsPopulation genetics and species delimitationEnvironmental, climatic and anthropogenic influences on marine predatorsEco-morphological evolution of seabirdsCollaborationJane has an extensive record of engagement with partner organisations, both locally and internationally. Her published and ongoing collaborations span government departments and institutes (CSIRO, Australian Antarctic Division, British Antarctic Survey, SAERI, Department of Natural Resources and Environment Tasmania), museums (TMAG, Field Museum of Natural History, American Museum of Natural History, Bell Museum), NGOs (Association Vahatra), and academic institutions (e.g. University of Bath, University of Chicago, Cornell University, University of Minnesota, University of Antananarivo, Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution, Pontificia Universidad Católica de Chile, Scripps Institution of Oceanography).Awards 2025: Young Tall Poppy, Australian Institute of Policy and Science 2025: Superstar of STEM, Science and Technology Australia 2018: Prize Fellowship, University of Bath2017: Doctoral Award, Royal Society of Tasmania2015: Endeavour Fellowship, Australian Government, Department of Education and Training2014: Allen Award, Australian Marine Sciences Association2011: Australian Postgraduate Award, University of Tasmania"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Jane Younger"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Jane.Younger"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Jane.Younger"^^xsd:string ;
