<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-jayne-knight-a2267cdd7c20> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Jayne Knight"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-humanities-873a000cedf1> ;
    utas:bio "Dr Jayne Knight is Senior Lecturer in Classics and Associate Head of School, Learning and Teaching Performance in the School of Humanities and Social Sciences. She researches the emotional culture of ancient Rome, with a particular focus on how emotions shaped politics and rhetoric in the late Republic and early Empire. Her monograph Anger and Politics in Ancient Rome (Bloomsbury, 2026) is the centrepiece of this program, building on her earlier work including ‘Anger as a Mechanism for Social Control in Imperial Rome’ in Emotion and Persuasion in Classical Antiquity (Franz Steiner Verlag, 2016), and connecting with forthcoming chapters on ‘Emotions in Roman Oratory’ in A Companion to Ancient Rhetoric: New Trends and Interdisciplinary Perspectives (De Gruyter, expected 2026) and ‘Senecan Strategies for Managing Envy in Early Imperial Rome’ for a planned volume on Envy in the Roman World (University of Michigan Press). Her entries on ‘emotions’ and ‘hatred’ in The Tacitus Encyclopedia (Wiley-Blackwell, 2023) further reflect her interest in the affective dimensions of Roman historical writing. A second strand of her research examines how Roman thought on politics and emotions was received and transformed in later periods. Her ARC-funded project on Machiavelli's engagement with Roman political theory informs a forthcoming chapter on ‘Anger and Discord in Machiavelli's Discourses on Livy’ in Machiavelli and the Anatomy of Passions (Brill). Her chapter ‘The Nature and Nurture of Kingship in Virgil's Georgics and Seneca's De Clementia’ in Latin Poetry and its Reception (Routledge, 2021) analyses Seneca's engagement with earlier Virgilian ideas and imagery to activate historical memory. Jayne believes that ancient texts speak to deep questions of human life, and one of the most rewarding things Classics can do is help students experience and appreciate the deep connections and gulfs between the ancient world and their own. She has taught ancient Roman history and culture and Latin language units at all levels. Her commitment to expanding access for students has driven sustained innovation in blended and online learning, recognised by multiple University of Tasmania Teaching Merit Certificates, a Student Focus Award, and a Team Citation for Outstanding Contributions to Student Learning. She has also contributed extensively to curriculum development at unit, major, and course level, including serving as Course Coordinator for the Bachelor of Arts with Honours and Assistant Dean, Distinctiveness and Innovation in the former College of Arts, Law and Education. Jayne is passionate about bringing the ancient world into public conversation, whether discussing Roman political culture, the lives of ancient people, or the ways that classical antiquity continues to shape how we think in the present. Alongside writing for general audiences in venues such as The Conversation and History Today, Jayne regularly gives public lectures and talks for community organisations, universities, and online audiences in Australia and internationally."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Jayne Knight"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Jayne.Knight"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Jayne.Knight"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Jayne.Knight@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
