<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-johanna-jones-c48c7b19d789> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Johanna Jones"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/university-of-tasmania-aef6a7aa1611> ;
    utas:bio "Dr Johanna Jones is a postdoctoral researcher at the Menzies Institute for Medical Research. After working in the education sector for several years, Johanna undertook additional study to complement her interests in health and human disease. She has a great interest in understanding how genetic variants disrupt normal cell biology and lead to disease. Johanna’s research is primarily focused on the genetics of childhood onset (congenital/paediatric) cataracts. She was awarded a PhD in 2020 from the University of Tasmania in the genetics of paediatric cataract. This successful body of work identified PGRMC1 as a novel isolated cataract gene, expanded the genotype-phenotype spectrum of known cataract genes, and has initiated investigations into the importance of structural and non-coding variants in cataract genetics. Johanna has honed a broad range of skills from sequencing and bioinformatics for variant identification and prioritisation, through numerous molecular biology techniques and use of zebrafish as a cataract disease model for variant validation and functional assessments. Johanna is also interested in developing in vitro assays to test the functional effects of putative disease-causing variants to improve genetic testing outcomes for patients. Johanna is passionate about undertaking research that will benefit patients and improve health outcomes for individuals with genetic diseases."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Johanna Jones"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Johanna.Jones"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Johanna.Jones"^^xsd:string ;
