<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-ke-liu-47e439f0a3d7> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Ke Liu"@en ;
    utas:bio "Dr Ke Liu has established an internationally recognised research profile in climate adaptation science and agricultural systems modelling. His work has significantly advanced the representation of soil waterlogging stress in many farming systems models, repositioning waterlogging from a historically under-represented agronomic constraint to a quantifiable and scenario-testable climate risk within mainstream process-based crop modelling frameworks. He led the development and implementation of generic waterlogging algorithms within internationally recognised modelling platforms (APSIM Classic/Next Generation, DSSAT and SWAGMAN Destiny), enabling cross-regional and scenario-based risk quantification. These tools have been adopted within collaborative modelling programs and decision-support frameworks used in more than 30 countries, supporting improved assessment of soil waterlogging stress in climate impact analyses. His 2023 Nature Communications paper, “Silver lining to a climate crisis in multiple prospects for alleviating crop waterlogging under future climates,” provided the first global-scale quantification of current and future waterlogging risk and mitigation potential and has helped catalyse broader research attention to waterlogging risk in climate impact assessments. This paper was featured by the journal under its Earth and Environmental Sciences Highlights and has been cited over 250 times and recognised as a Highly Cited Paper in the Web of Science. In recognition of the study’s significant impact, Dr. Liu was invited by the Nature editorial team to create a popular science video in 2024. The video has garnered more than 15,000 views on YouTube, reflecting the broad interest and influence of his research within the scientific community. Dr Liu has also been invited to co-lead the AgMIP waterlogging initiative, a leading international platform dedicated to harmonising soil waterlogging stress representation across crop models. This collaboration culminated in a Nature Food review, “Gaps and strategies for accurate simulation of waterlogging impacts on crop productivity”, published in 2025, which identified key knowledge gaps and strategic pathways for advancing waterlogging simulation and modelling standards internationally, further advancing the global understanding and modeling of waterlogging effects on agricultural systems. Since 2019, Dr Liu has published more than 110 refereed papers (35+ as first or corresponding author), ranked the 5th among the top 500 authors at the University of Tasmania (2022–2025) by scholarly output, with 4, 200+ citations and H-index of 37, Stanford/Elsevier's Top 2% Scientist Rankings (2025). Most of the papers published in prestigious journals such as Nature Food (2026, 2025, 2024) and Nature Communications (2026, 2023). These high-impact contributions reflect Dr. Liu’s strong commitment to research excellence, cross-disciplinary collaboration, and real-world impact, solidifying his reputation as a thought leader in the field of sustainable agriculture. Awards 2025: Agronomy Young Investigator Award, for early-career researcher who has made a significant contribution to the advancement of agronomy and agroecology. 2025: Plants Travel Awards for 3rd International Crop Modelling Symposium. 2025: The Tasmanian STEM Early Career Researcher Award 2025 (finalist), for Tasmania’s leading early career researchers in science, technology, engineering, and mathematics (STEM). 2025: Tasmanian 2025 Young Tall Poppy, for early-career researchers who are making a significant impact in science and also going out of their way to communicate their science to the broader community. 2024: Wheat Initiative Early Career Travel Awards, 3rd International Wheat Congress, Perth, WA.2024: Agronomy 2023 Outstanding Reviewer Award2023: Vice-Chancellor's Sustainability Award2022: The CoSE Executive Dean’s Award for Outstanding Performance by Recent HDR Graduate"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Ke Liu"^^xsd:string ;
