<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-lieke-scheepers-66d8cc30b9e5> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Lieke Scheepers"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/menzies-institute-for-medical-research-12e6f452c508> ;
    utas:bio "Dr Lieke Scheepers is a Research Fellow in the Environmental and Respiratory Health group at the Menzies Institute for Medical Research, University of Tasmania. She is an epidemiologist specialising in how early‑life environmental exposures influence health and development from pregnancy through to childhood and later life. Her research focuses on a range of environmental exposures, including ambient air pollution, wildfire smoke, greenness, and pesticide exposure. Using a life‑course epidemiology approach, her work examines how these exposures affect pregnancy outcomes, birth, and long‑term health trajectories. Her research draws on large cohort studies and data linkage to generate robust evidence grounded in real‑world health outcomes, to inform prevention strategies and public‑health policy. Dr Scheepers is the Lead of the Early‑Life Follow‑up (ELF) Stream of the Hazelwood Health Study, where she investigates the long-term cardiorespiratory, health and development of children exposed to a severe pollution event associated with a coal mine fire during their first 1000 days of life. She also provides scientific and operational leadership for the Statins Against Bushfire Smoke (SABS) clinical trial, which is testing whether interventions can reduce the cardiovascular impacts of wildfire smoke exposure. She is always interested in hearing from motivated Honours and PhD students who would like to work on environmental exposures and early‑life health using large population datasets. Doctor of Philosophy (PhD), Epidemiology, Maastricht University, The Netherlands, 2017.Master of Public Health - Epidemiology, Maastricht University, The Netherlands, 2012Bachelor of Engineering, The Hague University of Applied Sciences, The Netherlands, 2010Professional memberships:International Society for Environmental Epidemiology - memberAustralian and New Zealand Bone and Mineral Society - member and Research comittee memberDOHaD (Developmental Origins of Health and Disease) ANZPac Society - member and Tasmanian representative"^^xsd:string ;
