<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-nicholas-hookway-d8077be80b2a> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Nicholas Hookway"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-social-sciences-8de08ca8c819> ;
    utas:bio "Dr Nicholas Hookway – sometimes known as the ‘happy sociologist’ – is Senior Lecturer in Sociology in the School of Social Sciences. Nick is a cultural sociologist whose research focuses on how social bonds, emotions and identity are being transformed by wider social change. He is internationally known for his research on kindness, everyday morality, sports charity, loneliness and digital methods. From Toyota to Cancer Council Tasmania, Nick’s research has led to successful partnerships with a range of local and national organisations. Nick has published in the best sociology journals in the world, including Sociology, British Journal of Sociology, The Sociological Review and Qualitative Research. His monograph Everyday Moralities: Doing it Ourselves in an Age of Uncertainty was awarded the prestigious Stephen Crook Memorial Prize (2020) for best book in Australian sociology. Nick is a leader in developing digital methods in sociology. His article on using blogs in social research has been cited over 1000 times. Nick was an elected member of The Australian Sociology Association (TASA) Executive Committee (Public Engagement Portfolio Leader) (2016–2020) and prior to this was the Co-Convenor of the Cultural Sociology TASA Thematic Group (2014–2016). He is currently an Associate Editor for the Journal of Sociology and serves on the Editorial Board of The Sociological Review, one of the world’s foremost sociology journals. Prior to this, he was an associate editor for Sociology, considered one of the world's best sociology journals. Nick is passionate about bringing sociological ideas to wider audiences. His research is regularly featured in local and national media outlets, including ABC Radio National, The Age, The Herald Sun and The New Daily. Nick was awarded the UTAS Vice-Chancellor’s Award for Outstanding Community Engagement (2016). Nick is currently the Graduate Research Coordinator for Sociology/Criminology, the School of Social Science Academic Lead for campus transition and is a member of the Northern Tasmanian Headspace Board. Outside work, Nick is a qualified tennis coach, average guitar player and Hawthorn Football Club and Roger Federer obsessive. Nick is current secretary of the Trevallyn Tennis Club and is a competitive runner and tennis player. He is the proud Dad of four: two young humans and two fur babies – Douggie the Dachshund and Cocoa the Tenterfield Terrier!"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Nicholas Hookway"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Nicholas.Hookway"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Nicholas.Hookway"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Nicholas.Hookway@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/human-society-4632b664cb39>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/sociological-methodology-and-research-methods-77c7020e8818>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/sociology-1f39199d63c4> ;
    utas:orcid "0000-0002-0187-4741"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:researchFieldsText "Sociological methodology and research methods; Sociology; Human society"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:school "School of Social Sciences"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:supervises <https://example.org/utas-research/project/12391> ;
    utas:title "Doctor"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:updatedWhen "2026-09-12T03:35:17.8763142+10:00"^^xsd:string .

<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-peter-booth-b8c87a9fee30> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Peter Booth"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-architecture-and-design-34d92f346705> ;
    utas:bio "Peter is a Lecturer in the School of Architecture & Design at the University of Tasmania, specialising in computational design, digital fabrication, and sustainable material-driven approaches to architectural innovation. As an Early Career Researcher (ECR), his research investigates how emerging digital workflows can more effectively integrate material behaviour, fabrication constraints, and sustainability principles to advance architectural practice and regional manufacturing capacity. His work particularly emphasises the potential of latent wood-based materials within digitally integrated design and manufactured workflows. Peter has more than two decades of combined professional and academic experience within the field of architecture. Since registering as an architect in 2005, he has worked across Tasmania, Melbourne, and London in roles that include Project Architect on residential, commercial, heritage, healthcare, and educational projects. In 2012, he co‑founded Pulp Studio Architecture, where he continues to serve as Director, contributing design leadership and technical expertise to residential and small‑scale commercial work. His academic qualifications include a Bachelor of Environmental Design and Bachelor of Architecture from the University of Tasmania, a Master of Advanced Architecture (Digital Tectonics) from the Institute for Advanced Architecture of Catalonia in Barcelona, and a PhD in Architecture from the University of Technology Sydney. His doctoral work aligned with the Transformative Technologies & Data Poetics research group and examined computationally integrated approaches to non‑standardised plantation hardwood."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Peter Booth"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Peter.Booth"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Peter.Booth"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Peter.Booth@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/architectural-design-ef2e54dfd2fb>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/architectural-science-and-technology-29ba26379a16>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/architecture-4ca303a3bf7d>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/built-environment-and-design-9b32c5b60d69>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/data-visualisation-and-computational-incl-parametric-and-generative-design-bfbfeb9cd978>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/design-a77de8a6daa1> ;
    utas:orcid "0000-0002-2926-1365"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:researchFieldsText "Data visualisation and computational (incl. parametric and generative) design; Architectural design; Architectural science and technology; Design; Built environment and design; Architecture"^^xsd:string ;
