<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-ryan-day-5012d20f42d7> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Ryan Day"@en ;
    utas:bio "Ryan Day is Senior Research Fellow in Marine Animal Physiology and Biochemistry at University of Tasmania’s Institute for Marine and Antarctic Studies. In 2003, he received a Bachelor of Arts - Biology from the University of Chicago. In 2006, he received a Bachelor of Science - Marine Biology from the University of Queensland and in 2012, he received his PhD in Zoology from the University of Queensland. Ryan has been a researcher at IMAS since 2012. One of his primary research areas are in measuring the impact of seismic surveys on marine invertebrates. In this field, his research has used quantification of acid-base physiology, haemolymph electrolyte and mineral homeostasis, immune function and oxidative stress to assess the health status of marine invertebrates including lobster, scallops and octopus to evaluate the impact of exposure to seismic air gun signals. The outcomes of this research have been incorporated by the federal regulatory body (NOPSEMA) for impact assessments to minimise harm to marine life. In his second primary research area, Ryan investigates approaches for improving invertebrate fishery practices and outcomes, with a current focus on the commercial scallop. This research developed in response to reports of large-scale mortalities within the scallop fishery. In conducting an assessment of haemolymph physiology, including biochemistry and immune function, his research aims to identify environmental stressors underlying mortality to provide fishery management with options for responding to bed die offs. In similar work with the Southern Rock Lobster fishery response, Ryan authored an industry best practices guide with recommendations on handling and water quality management. Implementation of these recommendations has been attributed to eliminating mass mortality events and halving mortality rates by industry partners. This work has recently been extended to the fishing vessel component of the industry and projects to apply this research to other marine invertebrate fisheries are in development. In addition to his recent work with invertebrates, Ryan’s PhD research focused on digestive physiology in marine fishes, in which he characterised digestive strategies in fishes that lack a stomach."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Ryan Day"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Ryan.Day"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Ryan.Day"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Ryan.Day@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/agricultural-veterinary-and-food-sciences-ef9b96058d4a>,
