<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-santosh-pagire-47c522cad4ec> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Santosh Pagire"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/university-of-tasmania-aef6a7aa1611> ;
    utas:bio "Dr Santosh Kisan Pagire is a Research Fellow at the University of Tasmania (Australia), working with Prof. Alex C. Bissember on multiple ARC-funded programs in light-driven catalysis. He also serves as Assistant Professor of Chemistry at Indian Institute of Technology, Banaras Hindu University (IIT–BHU), Varanasi (India). Born in Maharashtra (India), Santosh’s academic trajectory transformed early in his BSc when disciplined mentorship from Prof. Dilip R. Thube and Prof. Tukaram S. Thopate ignited his passion for synthetic organic chemistry, particularly for organic reaction mechanisms. Rising from a below-average student to one of the top three in his class at the New Arts, Commerce, and Science College, Parner, he also ranked 5th in Chemistry across all colleges under the University of Pune in 2009. During his Master’s degree at the Department of Chemistry, University of Pune (2009–2011), he received the Eklavya (merit) and Kawathekar (need-cum-merit) fellowships. In 2010, he qualified the MH-SET (Maharashtra State Eligibility Test) on his first attempt, and in 2011, he secured a highly competitive All-India Rank of 22 (top 1%) in the CSIR–NET examination—again on the first attempt. He trained under distinguished teachers, including Prof. Murzban S. Wadia, Prof. Mukund G. Kulkarni, and Prof. Shridhar R. Gadre, with exceptional mentorship from Prof. Dilip D. Dhavale FASc (Head and Distinguished Professor, Department of Chemistry, University of Pune). Immediately after his Master’s in 2011, Santosh dedicated a few months to teaching chemistry free of charge to rural students at Dhokeshwar Mahavidyalaya in Maharashtra (Affil. University of Pune)—an experience that strengthened his communication, empathy, and commitment to accessible science education. In 2011–2013, he undertook early research training in asymmetric organocatalysis, international metal catalysis work with Prof. Pher G. Andersson (Sweden) and Prof. Thavendran Govender (South Africa), and advanced total-synthesis training at NCL Pune (India) with Dr Subhash P. Chavan. This broad foundation led to multiple international PhD offers, independent of fellowship outcomes. In 2013, he secured the prestigious DAAD Fellowship—on his first attempt—to pursue a PhD in Synthetic Organic Chemistry at the University of Regensburg (Germany) under Prof. Oliver Reiser, completing his doctorate in December 2017. Following his PhD, Santosh obtained the postdoctoral positions he most aspired to, and once again the associated elite international fellowships were awarded to his research proposals on the first attempt. His postdoctoral research included:• DFG Walter Benjamin Postdoctoral Fellow, BIKAKEN (Affil. The University of Tokyo, Japan) with Profs. Masakatsu Shibasaki and Naoya Kumagai (2018–2021);• Marie Skłodowska–Curie Postdoctoral Fellow, University of Bristol—ranked #1 in the UK for Chemistry (REF 2021)—with Prof. Varinder K. Aggarwal FRS (2021–2023);• followed by a research stay with Prof. Lutz Ackermann, University of Göttingen, Germany (2023–2024). In 2024–2025, he transitioned to industry, leading a Discovery Chemistry group at Syngene International Ltd., India, for Kestrel Therapeutics (USA), contributing to early-stage drug discovery. In March 2025, he joined Chanakya University, where his clear, application-focused teaching approach transformed initially uninterested BTech engineering students into highly engaged learners—earning him the highest teaching feedback ratings across the cohort. He moved to the University of Tasmania (Australia) in September 2025 to resume his research journey. Santosh’s research integrates visible- and near-infrared (NIR) photocatalysis, radical chemistry, asymmetric synthesis, and phosphorus chemistry to develop selective and sustainable molecular transformations. He has published in leading journals, including Accounts of Chemical Research (IF ~24.6), Angewandte Chemie International Edition (16.8), Journal of the American Chemical Society (16.4), and ACS Catalysis (13.7), and has accumulated >3,300 citations, with a strong first-author record in top-tier Q1 journals."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Santosh Pagire"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Santosh.Pagire"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Santosh.Pagire"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Santosh.Pagire@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/chemical-sciences-f63fef697a5b>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/inorganic-chemistry-5a940e9def83>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/organic-chemical-synthesis-298aab89070e>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/organic-chemistry-13920342f13f>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/organometallic-chemistry-0260146c43b8>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/photochemistry-537ba004859d>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/physical-chemistry-0847d24f9c4b> ;
    utas:orcid "0000-0002-8917-4933"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:researchFieldsText "Organic chemical synthesis; Photochemistry; Organometallic chemistry; Organic chemistry; Chemical sciences; Physical chemistry; Inorganic chemistry"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:school "University of Tasmania"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:supervises <https://example.org/utas-research/project/12075> ;
    utas:title "Doctor"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:updatedWhen "2026-09-12T03:36:22.5668680+10:00"^^xsd:string .

