<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-tamieka-pearce-a325fb6b95ba> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Tamieka Pearce"@en ;
    utas:bio "Dr. Tamieka Pearce is a Research Fellow at the Tasmanian Institute of Agriculture (TIA), specialising in genetics and molecular plant pathology. Her work underpins the productivity and sustainability of Tasmania’s horticultural industries. Since completing her PhD in 2016, she has focused on plant disease research in crops including pyrethrum, poppies, cherries, and blueberries. Her research has attracted more than $3.6 million in funding, with a third of this secured through projects she has led as Chief Investigator. Tamieka currently leads a pyrethrum flowering physiology research program aimed at reducing the crop growing season from 18 months to 10 months. This program integrates the identification of early-flowering plants, assessment of heritability, development of tissue culture techniques, and the use of genomic and transcriptome analyses to identify candidate genes in the juvenile–adult transition pathway. In 2023, she was awarded an ARC Early Career Industry Fellowship to advance this research, becoming the only UTAS recipient in the inaugural fellowship round. In addition to her ARC Fellowship, Tamieka leads an Agricultural Development Fund project (NRE TAS) developing a rapid molecular method for detecting fungal pathogens in pyrethrum leaves. She also supervises three PhD students. She was selected for the 2023 CoSE Future Research Leaders Program and the 2025 Sciences and Engineering Elevate Program. In 2024, she received both the CoSE Early Career Researcher Award and the Vice-Chancellor’s Early Career Researcher Award. She is also an active member of TIA’s Inclusion, Diversity, and Equity Committee. Beyond her professional achievements, Tamieka is committed to promoting diversity in agricultural science and to inspiring the next generation of researchers. She balances her career with running a cattle stud with her partner, Lachy, and their two young boys, Austin and Harvie, at their family farm"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Tamieka Pearce"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Tamieka.Pearce"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Tamieka.Pearce"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Tamieka.Pearce@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/agricultural-biotechnology-abeb569b288f>,
