<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-william-connelly-38138c0a2f1c> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor William Connelly"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/tasmanian-school-of-medicine-23cd72f7cf04> ;
    utas:bio "Highlights After more than a decade studying the computations performed by biological neural circuits, Dr William Connelly's research has shifted from understanding intelligence in the brain to building intelligent systems. His work now focuses on applied artificial intelligence and machine learning, developing models that solve practical problems in medicine, neuroscience and conservation. His research spans computer vision, deep learning, time-series analysis and explainable AI, with applications including human movement analysis, medical decision support, and embedded AI systems for wildlife conservation. Recent projects include the development of edge AI systems capable of identifying Tasmanian devils in the field to support automated vaccine delivery for the conservation of this endangered species. Dr Connelly's interest in machine learning grew directly out of his background as a neuroscientist. Having spent years studying biological neural networks, he was fascinated by the idea of constructing artificial ones: systems whose internal computations could be examined and understood in complete detail. Training his first neural network to recognise handwritten digits was a turning point. Watching a collection of simple mathematical operations learn to solve a problem without being explicitly programmed convinced him that machine learning would become one of the defining technologies of modern science. Since then, he has applied AI across a diverse range of biomedical and ecological problems, combining a strong quantitative background with practical experience developing, training and deploying deep learning models. His current research emphasises building reliable AI systems that can operate outside the laboratory, from wearable health technologies to low-power embedded devices deployed in remote environments. BiographyDr William Connelly completed his PhD at the University of Otago, NZ, where he used electrophysiology to study inhibitory neurotransmission in the central nervous system. He then took up a post-doctoral position with Prof. Vincenzo Crunelli, Cardiff University, UK, where he worked on the physiology of thalamocortical neurons. He then took up a position working with Prof Greg Stuart, Australian National University, where he worked on trying to understand synaptic integration in the visual cortex.In 2018 he took up a Lectureship at the University of Tasmania."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor William Connelly"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "William.Connelly"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/William.Connelly"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "William.Connelly@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/biomedical-and-clinical-sciences-dd47e129275f>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/information-and-computing-sciences-402af2692418>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/machine-learning-dcaa8e5ecb8d>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/neurosciences-658af4d53a9d>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/sensory-systems-e5c7f361f3d0> ;
    utas:orcid "0000-0002-0986-6260"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:researchFieldsText "Sensory systems; Machine learning; Neurosciences; Biomedical and clinical sciences; Information and computing sciences"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:school "Tasmanian School of Medicine"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:supervises <https://example.org/utas-research/project/12219> ;
    utas:title "Doctor"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:updatedWhen "2026-09-17T10:01:53.4059626+10:00"^^xsd:string .

<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-william-reay-585d3b7baec2> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor William Reay"@en ;
    utas:bio "Dr William Reay is a senior research fellow in Statistical Genetics and leads the Neurodegeneration and Psychiatric Genomics Research Group at the Menzies Institute for Medical Research. He currently holds a highly competitive NHMRC EL1 Investigator Fellowship (2024-2028). Dr Reay moved to Hobart from The University of Newcastle in 2024 where he played an important role in driving the genetics research within the Precision Medicine Research Program.Dr Reay is motivated to use genomics and other forms of big data to better understand and treat complex human disorders. He seeks to work at the interface of discovery and translational research, with his work on genetics informed treatment selection already proceeding to current clinical trials. Through this research, Dr Reay works with many stakeholders including researchers, clinicians, and consumers across a variety of disease areas, with a particular interest in psychiatric and neurological diseases. In particular, Dr Reay's research focus includes:1. Using genomics to identify new drug targets and opportunities to repurpose existing drugs for new uses2. Enhancing how genomics can better inform nutritional interventions 3. Linking common and rare forms of genetic risk factors4. Improved prediction of treatment response and side-effects5. Leveraging genetics to better understand the biological mechanisms that link different disordersDr Reay has been a CI on >8 million AUD in competitive funding despite and his work has already shown progress on the path to rapid translation. He is also currently the Lead Researcher of the Therapeutic Development Objective for the NHMRC funded Centre of Research Excellence in Multiple Sclerosis Translation. His scholarly work has been highly cited in a short time and he has led studies in some of the highest-impact journals in biomedical research including Circulation, Nature Reviews Genetics, The American Journal of Human Genetics, Nature Communications, and Science Advances."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor William Reay"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "William.Reay"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/William.Reay"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "William.Reay@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/bioinformatics-and-computational-biology-a4ee71add70f>,
