    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/university-of-tasmania-aef6a7aa1611> ;
    utas:bio "Alex Bissember is a Professor of Chemistry at the University of Tasmania. His research interests span areas of organic, organometallic, inorganic, and natural products chemistry and chemical education. Alex completed his PhD with Prof. Martin Banwell at ANU in 2010. He was awarded the 2009 American Australian Association Merck Company Foundation Fellowship, which supported postdoctoral research with Prof. Greg Fu at MIT and Caltech. In 2013, Alex commenced his independent career at the University of Tasmania. He is a former ARC Future Fellow.QUALIFICATIONSPhD (Chemistry), ANU, 2010BSc. (1st Class Hons Chemistry), ANU, 2005BSc. (Chemistry & Physics majors), ANU, 2004AWARDS, HONOURS & FUNDING2026–2028 ARC Discovery Project Grant, DP260100139 2025–2027 ARC Discovery Project Grant, DP250100285 2023 Royal Society of Tasmania M. R. Banks Medal2023 Royal Society of Chemistry Chemical Communications Pioneering Investigator2023–2026 ARC Linkage Project Grant, LP2201002522021 Elected a Fellow of the Royal Australian Chemical Institute2021–2024 ARC Discovery Project Grant, DP2101000252021–2025 ARC Future Fellowship, FT2001000492020 ARC Discovery Project Grant, DP2001017142020 Royal Australian Chemical Institute Alan Sargeson Lectureship2019 Royal Society of Chemistry Dalton Transactions New Talent: Asia-Pacific2019 University of Tasmania Vice Chancellor’s Innovation Award2019 University of Tasmania College of Sciences & Engineering Innovation Award2018 University of Tasmania College of Sciences and Engineering Executive Dean’s Award for Development and Innovation in Learning and Teaching2017 Royal Australian Chemical Institute Organometallic Chemistry Award2017 Minister’s Tasmanian STEM Innovation of the Year AwardFinalist, 2017 Tasmanian STEM Young Researcher of the Year Award2017 University of Tasmania Vice Chancellor’s Award for Early Career Academic Teaching2017 Collier Charitable Fund Grant2016 University of Tasmania Citation for Outstanding Contributions to Student Learning2015 PhosAgro/ UNESCO/ IUPAC Green Chemistry for Life Grant2013 University of Tasmania Faculty of Science Engineering & Technology Early Career Researcher Award2009 American Australian Association Merck Company Foundation FellowshipAPPOINTMENTSPresident, Southern Highlands Conference on Heterocyclic Chemistry (2024)Vice President, Southern Highlands Conference on Heterocyclic Chemistry (2023)Member of College of Assessors, New Zealand Ministry of Business, Innovation and Employment (since 2021)Australian Council for Undergraduate Research Steering Group Member (since 2020)Editorial Board Member, Scientific Reports (2015–2021)RACI National Organic Division Treasurer (2019–2022)RACI Diversity and Inclusion Committee Chair (2019–2020)RACI Organic Division Representative for Tasmania (since 2017)Visiting Associate, California Institute of Technology (2015)Postdoctoral Research Fellow, California Institute of Technology (2012–2013)Postdoctoral Research Fellow, Massachusetts Institute of Technology (2010–2012)AVAILABLE PHD PROJECTS- New Catalysts for Chemical Synthesis: project- Total Synthesis of Alkaloid Natural Products- Development of Original Photoredox-Catalysed Reactions: project- Development of Original Electrosynthetic Reactions: - Mechanistic Studies of Metal-Catalysed Cross-Coupling Reactions"^^xsd:string ;
