<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/professor-vaughan-higgins-30bbe5ffedaf> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Professor Vaughan Higgins"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-humanities-and-social-sciences-6fb206dd83ce> ;
    utas:bio "Vaughan Higgins is a Professor of Sociology and Head of the Sociology and Criminology Program in the School of Humanities and Social Sciences. Since graduating from his PhD in 2001, Vaughan’s research has focused on the forms of governance – including policies, programs, standards, technologies – and organisational arrangements that contribute to more sustainable and secure agri-food industries in Australia. Informed by analytical approaches such as assemblage thinking, actor network theory, and governmentality, Vaughan’s work has been important in highlighting the often nuanced and subtle ways in which agri-food governance is navigated and made to work in local and regional contexts. For the past 15 years, much of Vaughan’s research has focused on Australia’s biosecurity system, with a particular interest in the implementation of a shared responsibility approach. His work in this area has examined the institutional logics shaping how diverse biosecurity stakeholders engage with shared responsibility, as well as the practices through which biosecurity compliance is made workable in farming regions. Vaughan’s research on biosecurity has informed national agricultural policy and strategy. His expertise has played a key role in shaping Australia’s biosecurity future through membership of the Federal Department of Agriculture, Fisheries and Forestry’s National Biosecurity Strategy Working Group and, more recently, the National Biosecurity Strategy Implementation Committee. Vaughan joined the University of Tasmania in January 2018 as an Associate Professor. He commenced his academic career in 2000 as an Associate Lecturer (part-time) at Monash University. In 2011 he was appointed as an Associate Professor in the School of Humanities and Social Science at Charles Sturt University. During his career, Vaughan has had several international engagements, including visiting appointments at Cardiff University and Imperial College London."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Professor Vaughan Higgins"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Vaughan.Higgins"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Vaughan.Higgins"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Vaughan.Higgins@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/human-society-4632b664cb39>,
