<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/associate-professor-taryn-noble-b58b73e8c54c> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Associate Professor Taryn Noble"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/institute-for-marine-and-antarctic-studies-bd99c2807811> ;
    utas:bio "Dr Taryn Noble is Associate Professor in Antarctic paleoclimatology at the Institute for Marine and Antarctic Studies. She is interested in understanding the ocean’s role in past climate change. Her research is focused on the interaction between the ocean and Antarctic Ice Sheet during periods of past climate warming; using geochemical approaches to reconstruct the semnsitivity of vulnerable marine-based glaciers in East Antarctica, and the implications of ice sheet change on biogeochemical processes in Southern Ocean.Taryn applies geochemical methods in seawater and marine sediment cores to reconstruct ocean circulation, sediment provenance and biological fluxes in the ocean. Paleoclimate records are useful because they can provide insight into processes and feedbacks in the climate system, which can aid the representation of physical processes in ice sheet and climate models.BiographyDr Taryn Noble moved to the University of Tasmania after earning her PhD at the University of Cambridge in Southern Ocean Paleoceanography. Her first post doctoral experience (2012-2015) with the CRC in Optimising Resource Extraction involved developing new geochemical tests for mine water prediction, and the characterisation of the mineral dust at mine sites.In 2016, Taryn joined the Institute for Marine and Antarctic Studies via a SIEF John Stocker Postdoctoral Fellowship, which allowed her to shift her research focus south to Antarctica, and establish Nd and Sr isotope methods in the laboratory. In 2019, she took up a continuing position at IMAS, teaching ‘Introduction to Oceanography’ and ‘Antarctic Climate Evolution and Past Extremes’ and supporting IMAS's Honours and Masters students as depty-coordinator. She was awarded an Australian Research Council Discovery Early Career Researcher Award held from 2022-2025.Career summaryQualificationsPhD Southern Ocean circulation and sediment sourcing. University of Cambridge 2012MSc in Environmental Geosciences The influence of pH and substrate availability on the stable carbon isotope composition of methane University of Bristol 2007Service rolesAustralian representative on the GEOTRACES Scientific Steering Committee (2022 onging) MNF Technology and Innovation Advisory Group (TIAG) - CSIRO (2024 onging)"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Associate Professor Taryn Noble"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Taryn.Noble"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Taryn.Noble"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Taryn.Noble@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/chemical-oceanography-04a6615aa89a>,
