<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-jessica-urwin-e056713d9bc9> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Jessica Urwin"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/university-of-tasmania-aef6a7aa1611> ;
    utas:bio "Jessica Urwin is Lecturer in Environmental History in the School of Humanities, College of Arts, Law and Education. Her research interests and expertise span nuclear history, Australian history, environmental history, colonial and imperial history, and environmental justice. Her award-winning doctoral research, completed at the Australian National University, explored the relationship between nuclear processes (including radium and uranium mining, nuclear weapons testing, and nuclear waste dumping) and colonialism in Australia. Her first monograph, Contaminated Country: Nuclear Colonialism and Aboriginal Resistance in Australia, was published in the University of Washington Press' Weyehaeuser Environmental Books series in September 2025 and an Australian edition is set to be published by Melbourne University Press in 2026. Jess is a member of the Australia and Aotearoa New Zealand Environmental History Network's steering committee, an editorial board member of Global Environment, and a Friend of the Centre for Environmental History at ANU. Research Interests Jess' research interests span across Australian scientific, environmental, political, and colonial history. Her immediate field of expertise is in Australian nuclear, colonial, and environmental histories, but she has also worked on historical research concerned with the Murray-Darling Basin and other 'watery spaces', oral history, global environmental history, and the history of science and scientific networks in Australia and beyond. Expanding upon her work into nuclear colonialism, Jess is interested in Australian mining history, and hopes to engage critically with the industry's environmental and colonial dimensions, from settlement through to the present. In particular, she is interested in the politics of mineral extraction, from understanding the 'roots' of settlers' engagements with mining (in particular of radioactive minerals), to tracing the 'routes' of mined minerals across the world, to grappling with mining's more recent interactions with Indigenous land rights and conservation debates. Teaching Summary Jess is passionate about providing inclusive and accessible education to her students. She has taught global imperial history, Australian colonial history, and global environmental history, and provided mentorship to undergraduate, Honours and PhD students. She is the current coordinator of HASS Honours, and unit coordinator of HUM410: Contemporary Humanities Research, HUM405: Advanced Reading Seminar, HUM419: Advanced Text-Based Reading Seminar, HAA106: Oral History, and HTA348: Global Environmental History (alongside A/Prof Alessandro Antonello)."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Jessica Urwin"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Jessica.Urwin"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Jessica.Urwin"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Jessica.Urwin@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/australian-history-cbe98520cfb9>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/environmental-history-a5a1beb3a2b5>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/historical-studies-73b4333db4c6>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/history-heritage-and-archaeology-5fcaeb3e547e>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/history-of-empires-imperialism-and-colonialism-dcc91c50c89a> ;
    utas:orcid "0000-0001-5044-947X"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:researchFieldsText "Environmental history; Australian history; History of empires, imperialism and colonialism; Historical studies; History, heritage and archaeology"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:school "University of Tasmania"^^xsd:string ;
