<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-ross-turner-dacc117daaa9> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Ross Turner"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-natural-sciences-7ce3a3316721> ;
    utas:bio "Ross Turner is a Lecturer in Mathematics and Physics at the University of Tasmania. He received the University of Tasmania's Vice Chancellor's Award in 2022 for his accomplishments as an early career researcher, and was a finalist for the Australian Museum Eureka Prize in the same year for his outreach work at QueersInScience. Ross has previously been awarded the prestigious Bok Prize by the Astronomical Society of Australia for his theoretical astrophysics research on the relativistic jet outflows from supermassive black holes. Ross leads the analytical modelling research stream in the University's Theoretical and Computational Astrophysics Group (physics/astrophysics), which develops the statistical tools to analyse radio astronomy observations from next-generation large sky surveys, including by the Square Kilometre Array. He employs his expertise in computational physics to model and interpret complex dynamical systems as part of cross-disciplinary research with the Australian Centre for Excellence in Antarctic Science (computational earth sciences) and the Climate Mathematics Group (applied mathematics)."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Ross Turner"^^xsd:string ;
