<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-sara-karacsony-414d94b54e25> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Sara Karacsony"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-nursing-dc807d459cc3> ;
    utas:bio "I am a nursing academic researcher in the fields of palliative care and aged care and an active member of the Transforming Research in Ageing (TRiA) group within the school of Nursing at the University of Tasmania. My research and teaching areas focus on workforce education and skills development in palliative care, and care and quality of life of older people. I joined the project team on the first Australian-funded research study into the benefits of the multisensory, psychosocial Namaste Care program for people living with advanced dementia in residential aged care facilities and continue to promote the program’s philosophy and principles into various contexts of care as the Australian champion for Namaste Care International. My focus is on developing skilled, compassionate palliative care nurses (and care staff) within aged care and nursing graduands who are invested in evidence-informed practice. I endeavour to set students up for success as nurses of the future by helping to transmit evidence-based knowledge, develop comprehensive assessment skills and encourage positive, empowering attitudes so that they can provide the care that health and social care providers, patients, residents, clients and their families expect. BiographySara’s background embraces both the Arts and Science. As a Classics graduate from the University of London, Sara worked in educational publishing before undertaking a Bachelor of Nursing at the University of Technology, Sydney and beginning her nursing career. During this time, she identified her interest in palliative care, from which specialisation she could explore the perenial questions surrounding living and dying, health and ill health, and what matters as we face ageing and dying. An output of Sara's Bachelor of Nursing studies was a chapter 'Spirituality in Palliative Care' written within a resource for nurse practitioners entering the speciality of palliative care. Sara’s clinical background is palliative care nursing across specialist, acute and community palliative care services. Sara’s PhD (2018) research focused on the residential aged care workforce and the development of an instrument to evaluate the knowledge, skills and attitudes of nursing assistants (unlicensed health care workers) providing a palliative approach to older people in residential aged care facilities. Sara joined the University of Tasmania as a Lecturer in Nursing in August 2017 and teaches into both the undergraduate and postgraduate programs."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Sara Karacsony"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Sara.Karacsony"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Sara.Karacsony"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Sara.Karacsony@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/aged-care-nursing-c060ae98434c>,
