<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-t-m-rabiul-islam-14292160c60d> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor T M Rabiul Islam"@en ;
    utas:bio "Dr. T M Rabiul Islam is a highly accomplished marine engineering academic and professional whose career seamlessly integrates extensive seagoing experience, educational excellence, and academic leadership. His professional journey began with hands-on experience as a marine engineer aboard oil tankers, where he gained invaluable practical insights into shipboard operations and the complexities of the global maritime industry, which continues to inform and enrich his teaching and research. Dr. Islam completed his PhD in Maritime Engineering with an “AA” rating at the Australian Maritime College (AMC), University of Tasmania, in 2017. His doctoral research excellence was recognised with the Highly Commended Rob Lewis Medal for outstanding postgraduate research. Prior to this, he earned an MPhil in Naval Architecture and Marine Engineering from the University of Strathclyde, Glasgow (UK) in 2013, and a Bachelor of Engineering (Honours) in Marine Engineering Technology from the University of Greenwich (UK) in 2009. His academic foundation was established with a Diploma in Marine Engineering Technology from the Bangladesh Institute of Marine Technology in 2005. Further strengthening his international research profile, Dr. Islam served as a Visiting Researcher at the Centre for Risk, Integrity and Safety Engineering (C-RISE) at Memorial University of Newfoundland, Canada, where he contributed to advanced research in risk assessment, integrity management, and safety engineering. Dr. Islam has held multiple academic and leadership roles at the Australian Maritime College, University of Tasmania. He commenced his academic career as a Casual Lecturer in 2017 before being appointed Lecturer in Marine Engineering at the National Centre for Ports and Shipping (NCPS) in 2018. Since 2021, he has served as Course Coordinator for the Bachelor of Applied Science (Marine Engineering) (Years 1 and 2), Pre-Sea (Engine), and Engineer Watchkeeper programs within the Centre for Seafaring and Maritime Operations (CSMO). He also contributed to academic leadership as Deputy Associate Head (Learning and Teaching) and CSMO Representative for Future Students from July 2024 to December 2025. Dr. Islam has been serving as Deputy Director – Ocean Seafaring within CSMO since September 2025. Through his leadership, strong industry engagement, and sustained commitment to academic excellence, he continues to make significant contributions to marine engineering education, maritime safety and reliability research, and the strategic development of maritime programs at AMC."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor T M Rabiul Islam"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Rabiul.Islam"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Rabiul.Islam"^^xsd:string ;
