<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-waqas-hassan-cef34a51379a> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Waqas Hassan"@en ;
    utas:bio "Dr Waqas Hassan is a Senior Lecturer in the School of Engineering and the Program Leader for Power Electronics and Control at the Centre for Renewable Energy and Power Systems (CREPS), University of Tasmania. He is the Graduate Research Coordinator (GRC) in the School of Engineering and the specialisation coordinator for the undergraduate (UG) Electrical Power and postgraduate (PG) Renewable Energy programs at UTAS. He is a Senior Member of IEEE and currently serves as the Chair of the IEEE Tasmania Subsection and the Counsellor of the IEEE Student Branch Tasmania, Australia. He also serves as an Associate Editor for the IEEE Transactions on Industry Applications. With a career spanning over 10 years in academia, research, and industry, he brings a distinctive blend of theoretical knowledge and practical engineering expertise to his role. Dr Hassan excels at analysing complex engineering problems and delivering practical, impactful solutions, particularly in the fields of electrical power and renewable energy systems. Dr Hassan's research interests focus on the design and development of renewable energy technologies and products, renewable power systems, power electronics, battery storage and grid integration, power system control and stability, solar photovoltaic systems, microgrids, hydrogen energy, motor drives, and electric vehicle systems. He leverages his expertise to develop innovative, sustainable energy solutions, addressing the world's growing energy demands through climate-safe engineering practices to enable a more resource-efficient and sustainable future. His work aims to transform our current electricity infrastructure into a smart, sustainable, and resilient system with zero emissions.Prior to joining the University of Tasmania, Dr Hassan worked as a senior engineer at Regal Rexnord Corporation, where he led power electronics research and design projects and successfully developed and commercialised new industrial products, including a next-generation power inverter, demonstrating his capability to bridge innovative research with practical industry implementation. Additionally, he has also served as a casual lecturer at Monash University, Australia. Following his PhD completion, Dr Hassan held a postdoctoral research position at the University of Technology Sydney (UTS), Australia. During his doctoral studies, he actively engaged with the academic community, working as a casual academic lecturer and tutor at the University of Sydney. Driven by his passion for teaching, he spent six years as a full-time university lecturer at Sir Syed CASE Institute of Technology, Pakistan. In this role, he coordinated and taught undergraduate and postgraduate courses, conducted research, and supervised honours and postgraduate students. His enduring passion for teaching and research continues to shape his contributions to the field of renewable energy and power systems."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Waqas Hassan"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Waqas.Hassan"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Waqas.Hassan"^^xsd:string ;
