<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/professor-kristy-de-salas-0c989f500a4a> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Professor Kristy de Salas"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/university-of-tasmania-aef6a7aa1611> ;
    utas:bio "Professor of Information and Communication Technology and Associate Head of Research at the University of Tasmania, Kristy de Salas is a distinguished academic and industry leader with a passion for leveraging technology for education, health, and social well-being. With a career spanning over two decades, she has established herself as a key figure in the Australian ICT landscape, recognised for her innovative research in serious games, human-computer interaction, and computing education. Professor de Salas's work is deeply rooted in the belief that technology can be a powerful force for positive change. Her research portfolio aligns with the UN Sustainable Development Goals, focusing on good health and well-being, quality education, and industry, innovation, and infrastructure. This commitment is reflected in her extensive list of publications and her active supervision of PhD and Masters students who are exploring cutting-edge topics in ICT. Beyond her academic achievements, Professor de Salas is a dedicated educator and mentor. She has received accolades for her teaching excellence and is known for her creative and collaborative approach to learning. Her dedication to fostering the next generation of ICT professionals is evident in her commitment to curriculum development and her active role in student supervision. A prominent advocate for the local tech industry, Professor de Salas has held numerous leadership positions, including Chair of Tasmanian Game Makers and a board member for various creative and technology-focused organisations. She is a sought-after speaker and has been instrumental in bridging the gap between academia and industry, fostering a vibrant and collaborative ICT community in Tasmania. Her expertise is frequently sought by government and industry bodies, including her role as an advisor to Screen Tasmania. Connect with Professor de Salas to learn more about her research, explore potential collaborations, or discover the exciting opportunities in Tasmania's thriving ICT sector."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Professor Kristy de Salas"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Kristy.DeSalas"^^xsd:string ;
