<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/professor-melanie-greenwood-7c6f79b94c27> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Professor Melanie Greenwood"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/university-of-tasmania-aef6a7aa1611> ;
    utas:bio "Professor Greenwood's focus is firmly located in supporting individuals to reach their optimal potential as graduates, academics and researchers. She brings extensive experience to learning and teaching, research supervision, mentorship of academics and clinicians and has significant critical care nursing expertise. Melanie undertakes research and supports higher degree students in the recognition and response to deteriorating patients across the lifespan within a quality and safety in health care focus. She is a life member of the Australian College of Critical Care Nurses and leads the Education Advisory Panel influencing standards for critical care specialty practice. She has received grants for establishing the Prevent Alcohol and Risk Related Trauma in Youth (P.A.R.T.Y. Hobart) program within Tasmanian Schools and teaching and learning grants for peer assisted teaching. Melanie has extensive collaborative associations with health care researchers across Australia.Melanie has an active role in the Transforming Research in Ageing (TRIA) group in the School of Nursing with a particular interest in sepsis in the older person. She is also active in Scholarship of Learning and Teaching research.BiographyPrior to joining the University of Tasmania, to coordinate postgraduate nursing courses, Melanie was a Nurse Educator in the Department of Critical Care Medicine at the Royal Hobart Hospital. She played a lead role in establishing the Medical Emergency Team system for recognising and responding to physiological deterioration in ward patients. Melanie has worked in critical care units in the United Kingdom and has undertaken postgraduate studies in neuroscience nursing at the Wessex Neurological Centre, University Hospital Southampton, a large tertiary referral centre for Southern England and the Channel Islands. She has a strong profile in curriculum design in postgraduate nursing courses, and the scholarship of learning and teaching.QualificationsPhD, University of Tasmania, Australia, 2018. A Critical Realist Descriptive Explanation of How Nurses Use Vital Signs in Acute CareMaster of Nursing, University of Tasmania, Australia, 2002. Thesis: Coming to know critical care practiceGrad Cert University Learning and Teaching, University of Tasmania, Australia, 2004BNurs (Post Registration), University of Tasmania, Australia, 1995Intensive Care Cert., Royal Hobart Hospital, Australia, 1987Neuroscience Cert., ENB148, England, 1984MembershipsLife Member of the Australian College of Critical Care Nurses (ACCCN)Chair of the Education Advisory Panel (ACCCN)Member of the National Education Committee (ACCCN)Committee member Tasmania Branch (ACCCN)Peer reviewer Australian Critical Care Journal and several othersMember of Higher Education Research and Development AssociationMember of Climate Action NursesAdministrative expertiseActing for Head of School of Nursing Postgraduate Course Advisor to Head of School of NursingDomain Campus Lead (Hobart) Lead Academic Integrity Adviser (School of Nursing)Associate Head Learning and Teaching responsible for Bachelor of Nursing and Postgraduate Nursing CoursesDirector Postgraduate Courses in the School of NursingCourse Coordinator Postgraduate Nursing CoursesShort course development (Leadership) Conference Convener for several national conferences (Crritical Care and also for Tasmanian Health Services)"^^xsd:string ;
