<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-james-venning-33d27a06a0b9> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor James Venning"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/australian-maritime-college-b2b959211c61> ;
    utas:bio "Dr James Venning is a researcher at the Australian Maritime College. He works in the Cavitation Research Laboratory studying how water can be torn apart by low pressure. This is a noisy, violent, phenomenon and yet beautiful at the same time.BiographyFollowing an undergraduate course in Aerospace Engineering, Dr Venning’s post-graduate research at Monash University into bluff-body wakes was the start of his work in experimental fluid mechanics. Using techniques such as Particle Image Velocimetry (PIV), hot-film anemometry, laser-doppler velocimetry, and novel force measurement tools, James has studied the flows around various bluff bodies, with a particular focus on vehicle aerodynamics. James has experience working in water flumes, wind tunnels, and cavitation tunnels, on both applied and fundamental research.Career summaryQualificationsPhD Vortex structures in the wakes of two- and three-dimensional bodies. Monash University Australia 2015Bachelor of Aerospace Engineering (1st Class Hons) The effect of forcing on flow over rectangular plates Monash University Australia 2010"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor James Venning"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "James.Venning"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/James.Venning"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "James.Venning@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/engineering-8c85b6639e62>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/fluid-mechanics-and-thermal-engineering-5ce49eb44566>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/marine-engineering-09c7667fc9b1>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/maritime-engineering-903545a5d7a9> ;
    utas:orcid "0000-0002-4097-2168"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:researchFieldsText "Fluid mechanics and thermal engineering; Marine engineering; Engineering; Maritime engineering"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:school "Australian Maritime College"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:supervises <https://example.org/utas-research/project/12209>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/project/12214>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/project/12401> ;
    utas:title "Doctor"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:updatedWhen "2026-09-12T03:36:05.0832643+10:00"^^xsd:string .

<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-karelle-siellez-4b4eeb29051f> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Karelle Siellez"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/university-of-tasmania-aef6a7aa1611> ;
    utas:bio "Dr Karelle Siellez is an astrophysicist and Lecturer in Physics at the University of Tasmania. Her research focuses on transient and multi-messenger astrophysics, including gamma-ray bursts, kilonovae, supernovae, gravitational-wave counterparts, and rapid optical follow-up of short-lived cosmic explosions. Her work connects high-energy space missions, gravitational-wave observatories, and ground-based telescope networks to understand some of the most energetic events in the Universe. Before joining UTAS, Dr Siellez contributed to major international collaborations including LIGO/Virgo and Fermi/GBM. She developed and led work on subthreshold gamma-ray burst searches associated with gravitational-wave events, contributing to the scientific context of the first joint gravitational-wave and electromagnetic detection, GW170817/GRB 170817A. This discovery transformed the field by confirming that neutron-star mergers can produce both gravitational waves and light, and that they are sites of heavy-element production in the Universe. As part of the LIGO Scientific Collaboration, she shared in the 2017 Breakthrough Prize in Fundamental Physics. At UTAS, Dr Siellez is developing a research, teaching and engagement program centred on southern-hemisphere transient astronomy. Her work aims to strengthen the role of Tasmanian optical facilities in rapid follow-up of gamma-ray bursts, kilonovae and gravitational-wave counterparts, while creating student pathways into real astronomical research. She is involved in national and international initiatives linked to SVOM, Rubin Observatory/LSST, OzGrav, robotic telescope networks and time-domain astronomy. Dr Siellez is also a committed science communicator who connects physics with storytelling, music, visual art and public engagement. She has delivered public talks and outreach activities across Australia, France, French Polynesia and the United States, including schools, festivals, public lectures and interdisciplinary science-art events. Her engagement work includes gravitational-wave sonification, astronomy-inspired painting, dark-sky advocacy, and public programs highlighting the scientific, cultural and environmental importance of Tasmania’s skies."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Karelle Siellez"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Karelle.Siellez"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Karelle.Siellez"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Karelle.Siellez@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/astronomical-instrumentation-87bbab6d2262>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/astronomical-sciences-ad8094126c48>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/cosmology-and-extragalactic-astronomy-448d060c48c7>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/general-relativity-and-gravitational-waves-c1bda3bc2953>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/high-energy-astrophysics-and-galactic-cosmic-rays-7877a02474c6>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/physical-sciences-1a7983f5d183> ;
    utas:orcid "0000-0002-8568-8523"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:researchFieldsText "General relativity and gravitational waves; High energy astrophysics and galactic cosmic rays; Cosmology and extragalactic astronomy; Astronomical instrumentation; Astronomical sciences; Physical sciences"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:school "University of Tasmania"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:supervises <https://example.org/utas-research/project/12024>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/project/12124>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/project/12342> ;
    utas:title "Doctor"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:updatedWhen "2026-09-12T03:35:55.7077248+10:00"^^xsd:string .

<https://example.org/utas-research/supervisor/doctor-melinda-mchenry-62f91c5f3091> a utas:Supervisor ;
    rdfs:label "Doctor Melinda McHenry"@en ;
    utas:affiliatedWith <https://example.org/utas-research/school/school-of-geography-planning-and-spatial-sciences-de1e76643cc9> ;
    utas:bio "I'm the Associate Head of School for Learning and Teaching in the School of Geography, Planning and Spatial Sciences. I lead a team of exceptional people who are experts in nature conservation, climate science, landscape restoration, measuring and monitoring the natural environment, and making planful decisions that enhance sustainability and life on land. Our team teach in exciting ways and want to bring opportunities to learners worldwide via Tasmania's landscapes as living laboratories, irrespective of whether this is online or here in Hobart. I'm an abiotic physical geographer who works in commodities (agriculture, forestry, mineral resources, geotourism) and nature (geo)conservation. My work is applied to geoheritage conservation and environmental education and extension. My contributions have been instrumental in advancing understanding of geoconservation theory and practise, and the management of geodiversity. I deliver Australia's sole undergraduate and postgraduate units in geoheritage and geotourism, bridging the gap between outdoor recreation, production landscapes and protected areas conservation practice. I'm passionate about bringing the cummulative expertise of my industry and practitioner networks into the classroom, where I use virtual and Tasmanian landscape exploration and professional training to build confidence, workforce capability and geographic knowledge. My research is all about geoconservation. I have built a team of geoconservation researchers and practitioners who use and develop Tools for Abiotic Research in Nature (TARN). We examine and protect the values of cultural landscapes, develop digital tools to support geoconservation protections in UNESCO Global Geoparks, and examine degradation risks from natural hazards to geodiversity and geoheritage in protected areas. The tools that we create are sometimes instruments or new technologies, and sometimes they are decision-support frameworks or new processes for people managing soils, waterways, landforms and heritage places. I have managerial and/or leadership expertise in industry, government and academia. My strengths are in the coordination of large, inter-disciplinary teams, change management, advocacy, strategy, and managing for diversity. I think it takes many different types of people to solve problems, and I encourage my teams to contribute their diverse talents alongside industry and community. There are collaborative approaches to solve problems that balance the needs of humans and nature, and I like to bring people together to make this happen."^^xsd:string ;
    utas:canonicalName "Doctor Melinda McHenry"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileId "Melinda.McHenry"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:discoveryProfileUrl "https://discover.utas.edu.au/api/users/Melinda.McHenry"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:email "Melinda.McHenry@utas.edu.au"^^xsd:string ;
    utas:hasResearchField <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/built-environment-and-design-9b32c5b60d69>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/conservation-and-biodiversity-c4eb69f5ad84>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/cultural-heritage-management-incl-world-heritage-541c4f5ba25d>,
        <https://example.org/utas-research/research_field/earth-sciences-32a57b471aa6>,
